[Event "Lasker – Bauer, Amsterdam 1889 (the double bishop sacrifice)"]
[White "Lasker"]
[Black "Bauer"]
[Date "1889.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Two bishops aimed at the king can be sacrificed on h7 and g7 to strip it bare. The first
famous double bishop sacrifice. The idea has been repeated in hundreds of games since.
Learn the pattern.} 1. f4 d5 2. e3 Nf6 3. b3 e6 4. Bb2 Be7 5. Bd3 b6 6. Nf3 Bb7 7. Nc3
Nbd7 8. O-O O-O 9. Ne2 c5 10. Ng3 Qc7 11. Ne5 Nxe5 12. Bxe5 Qc6 13. Qe2 a6 14. Nh5 Nxh5
15. Bxh7+ {Question: Black's knight just took on h5. Both your bishops point at the king.
What now? 15.Bxh7+! Kxh7 16.Qxh5+ Kg8 17.Bxg7! The second bishop goes too. The engine
rates it about +5.7.} 15... Kxh7 16. Qxh5+ Kg8 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 18. Qg4+ Kh7 19. Rf3 {The
rook lift: the rook swings to h3 for mate threats. Black must give the queen back.} 19...
e5 20. Rh3+ Qh6 21. Rxh6+ Kxh6 22. Qd7 {A queen fork wins one of the black bishops. Lasker
is now material up as well.} 22... Bf6 23. Qxb7 Kg7 24. Rf1 Rab8 25. Qd7 Rfd8 26. Qg4+ Kf8
27. fxe5 Bg7 28. e6 Rb7 29. Qg6 f6 30. Rxf6+ Bxf6 31. Qxf6+ Ke8 32. Qh8+ Ke7 33. Qg7+ Kxe6
34. Qxb7 Rd6 35. Qxa6 d4 36. exd4 cxd4 37. h4 d3 38. Qxd3 1-0
